A||N||7||U Wanna Race||Wax Build Up||||8||
Z||000000||U Wanna Race||10-11-2001||11:24 PM||||I bought my red 85 coupe about 2 years ago.  When I got it the paint was great.  I waxed and washed my car religiously.  At the beginning of this summer I noticed parts of my paint, specifically my roof, decklid, and the hood looked like they were oxidizing but yet layers of white could be scratched off.  So I thought it was a wax build up.  So I busted out the polish and rubbing compound and the paint looked great for a day but then it went to its nasty white color after that.  I am lost as to what to do next.  Any help would be greatly appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>I will race you for Pinks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Mach10||10-12-2001||12:37 AM||||Sounds like your clearcoat is coming apart  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Only thing I can suggest is break out the rubbing compound and try to work it below the oxidization. Don't wax it, just cut it down. Try a bottle of de-wazer, and rub down again... Good luck!||||reg||9
Z||000002||U Wanna Race||10-12-2001||12:43 AM||||So now that my clear coat is gone....this pretty much means I need a new paint job right?  Or can I manage putting on a clear coat to the affected areas and having it look nice when its all done?  And if or when I decide to get it painted could I use some chemical say a paint thinner or something to take my paint off without sanding?||||reg||11
Z||000003||Mach10||10-12-2001||03:07 AM||||Unfortunately, pretty much. However, you might be able to get away with a new clear-coat. Go speak to some detailing shops. They might be able to fine-sand the clear-coat down to the basecoat, and re-apply. Won't cost AS much as a good new paint job...<P>On the other hand, you can just do what I did, and have Maaco do an "Ambassador" enamel paint job. I sanded myself, and prepped. Paint looks great, and is holdin well. Not that I care $240 + tx, and I want it to last 1 year. If it does, I'm happy. The panels get stripped and off-car painted around then, anyway...<P>Don't use a stripper on the panels. You run the risk of melting down the resins of the panel... Go talk to some body-guys, and see if they have a stripper that will not each the enduraflex. Otherwise, lube up your elbows  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||10-12-2001||01:33 PM||||sanding and new paint (basecoat color and clear) is only way out. Just sanding and clearing is just temporary at best, clear wont stick to old base, only to freshly applied basecoat. Thats part of the problem with basecoat delamination, too long between color and clear. Middle 80's cars were the very worst. (remember all fieros are from 84-88)||||reg||1
Z||000005||U Wanna Race||10-12-2001||03:46 PM||||Damn...Guess I need to spend some more cash.  Broke right now though because my tranny siezed up on me along with my engine.  That cost be about 3000 and that was with me doing all the labor.  I swear my car thinks I am made of money or something.  Ohh well its worth it when you dust some rice burner.||||reg||9
